id: rust.cancellation
kind: rust
title: Cancellation
aliases: cancellation, cancellation token, abort
tags: async, resilience, shutdown
questions:
  - how do you cancel async work safely in Rust?
invariant: Futures are cancelled by being dropped, so cancellation is cooperative and every await is a cancellation point.
answer_30s: Dropping a future cancels it, so the standard patterns are a CancellationToken or a watch flag raced in select!, which lets work stop at a safe point instead of mid-write -- and cleanup steps must be idempotent, because a cancelled write may already have happened.
answer_2m: |
  The mechanics differ from other languages: there is no external interrupt, and
  dropping stops the future at its next await, so any partially applied side
  effect stays applied. That is precisely why steps must be idempotent and why
  cancellation points belong before, not during, a write. The toolkit:
  CancellationToken (propagates to children, races well in select!),
  JoinHandle::abort for the blunt case, watch channels for flags. Graceful
  shutdown is the disciplined version: stop accepting, signal cancellation, wait
  for in-flight work under a timeout, then exit.
when_to_use:
  - shutdown, timeouts, deadlines, abandoning work whose result nobody needs
avoid_when:
  - cancelling work with partial side effects that is not idempotent
minimal_example: |
  use tokio_util::sync::CancellationToken;

  let token = CancellationToken::new();
  let child = token.child_token();

  tokio::spawn(async move {
      tokio::select! {
          biased;
          _ = child.cancelled() => tracing::info!("cancelled"),
          r = do_work() => tracing::info!(?r, "finished"),
      }
  });

  token.cancel();
follow_ups:
  - how do you shut down without dropping in-flight requests?
related:
  - rust.tokio-select
  - design.graceful-shutdown
  - design.timeouts
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/tokio-util/latest/tokio_util/sync/struct.CancellationToken.html
